        <body>
            <div xml:id="d265">

                <pb facs="0680" n="590" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg590"/>

                <head>No. 261.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">General Schenck to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-02-05"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 5, 1875</date>.
                        (Received Feb. 20.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 693.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>. Parliament assembled today, the session
                    being opened, not by the Queen in person, but, as has been done for many years,
                    by royal commission. I send herewith a copy of Her Majesty’s speech on the
                    occasion, which shadows out no new or extraordinary policy, domestic or foreign.
                    But in one clause we may come to have an interest, as referring to something
                    which is intended probably to affect the terms on which certain relations maybe
                    hereafter arranged between Great Britain and the United States. Among the
                    measures to be laid by the present government before Parliament is mentioned “a
                    bill for the amendment of the merchants’ shipping acts.” * * *</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>ROBT. C. SCHENCK.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d265-a1.xml" xpointer="d265-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
